course: dictionary
lesson: 14875
title: Rearrangement: changing the order
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A rearrangement is a change in the order, position or plan of things. When you meet this word, look at the words around it to find what has changed. «The class made a rearrangement of the desks before the test.» The desks were moved into a new order or position.
explain: The same word can be used for a changed time or date. «The school made a rearrangement of the sports day, so it is now on Friday.» Nothing was bought or broken. The plan was changed. Ask yourself: what was moved, changed or re-ordered?
explain: In maths, a rearrangement changes how a formula is written without changing what it means. «Rearrange a = b + c to make b the subject» gives b = a − c. The parts have moved to new places, but the rule is still true. Check your answer by putting numbers in both versions.
fact: A rearrangement means changing the order, position or plan of things.
fact: «A rearrangement of the furniture» means moving the sofa, chairs or tables into new places.
fact: «A rearrangement of the match» means the match has a new time or date.
fact: In maths, a rearrangement puts the parts of a formula in a different order, for example a = b + c can become b = a − c.
fact: Rearrangement is often used with the words «furniture rearrangement» and «date rearrangement».
exercise: «The teacher made a rearrangement of the chairs for the play.» What is a rearrangement here? | A list of the children's names | A broken chair | *A new order or position for the chairs | A loud noise
exercise: Rearranging a = b + c to find b gives which new formula? | b = a + c | a = b − c | b = c − a | *b = a − c
exercise: «The football match has a new date, so the club made a rearrangement.» What does rearrangement mean here? | A long walk to the pitch | A new football | A goal scored | *A changed plan or date
test: «The shop made a rearrangement of its shelves.» What does rearrangement mean? | Shelves taken away for ever | Shelves bought from a new shop | *Shelves moved into a new order | Shelves painted red
test: Rearranging the letters of «tap» can make which word? | fish | bed | *pat | cup
exam: Which best describes a rearrangement? | Counting to ten slowly | Buying something brand new | *Changing the order or position of things | Breaking things into small pieces
